export const rest = {
  "PS-01|The PBS approach": [
    "Positive behaviour support aims at a life the person wants: relationships, something to do, and control over the day. A plan that only says what to do when they are distressed has missed the point. Proactive strategies change the environment, the demand, or the communication so a crisis is less likely.",
    "The practitioner assesses and writes the plan with the person and with the people who will use it. Your role is to follow it as written and to report what actually happens. You do not invent a new consequence on a hard shift and call it the plan.",
  ],
  "PS-01|Reading a plan": [
    "An interim plan covers a short, defined period while the full assessment is done. A comprehensive plan is the fuller one. Know which you are holding. An interim plan that has quietly become permanent is something you raise, not a convenience you keep using.",
    "Read the proactive strategies, the early signs, and the response strategies before you need them. If a restrictive practice is included, read when it may be used, for how long, and what you must record. If you cannot find those conditions, you do not use the practice.",
  ],
  "PS-01|Implementing": [
    "The plan works when the whole team does the same thing. One worker who adds a punishment, or who skips the proactive step, makes the response random. Talk about the actual steps at handover.",
    "Change the environment and the routine the way the plan says: a warning before a transition, a smaller demand at the hard time of day, the sensory item within reach. De-escalation is space, a lower voice, and a smaller request. Keep yourself safe. Leaving to get help is part of the plan, not abandoning the person.",
  ],
  "PS-01|Data and review": [
    "ABC recording is what happened before, what the person did, and what happened after, including what you did. Write it soon. 'Had a behaviour' tells the practitioner nothing they can use.",
    "Report strategies that are not being used, including when the roster makes them impossible. Authorised use of a regulated restrictive practice is reported monthly. Unauthorised use is an incident and is reported much sooner. You do not wait for the monthly tally.",
  ],
  "PS-02|Plans and where they come from": [
    "The NDIS plan is what is funded. The service agreement is what your organisation and the person agreed you will do. The support plan is how that looks in the week: goals, preferences, routines, risks, and communication. The roster is none of those.",
    "Read the parts that change a shift. How they communicate, what and how they eat, medication, what frightens them, and what they want more of. A plan you have not read will be replaced by your habits.",
  ],
  "PS-02|Reading the plan": [
    "Before the first shift, read it and find what matters today. A medical appointment outranks a cooking goal. A mealtime texture does not wait for a quiet day.",
    "Health, medication, mealtime, behaviour, and manual handling sections are safety. If a section is missing and the support clearly needs it — they cough when they drink, they have seizures, they need a hoist — ask before you improvise.",
  ],
  "PS-02|Delivering against it": [
    "Link the shift to something in the plan. If the goal is making a drink, build the drink into the morning. If they want something different today, follow that preference and record it. The plan guides you. It does not cancel their choice.",
    "Write what you did and how they responded. A note that could have been written without meeting them records attendance, not support.",
  ],
  "PS-02|Reviewing": [
    "A plan is stale when the person has changed and the paper has not: a new skill, a new risk, a goal they have met, a strategy nobody uses. You will see this on shift before a review date arrives.",
    "Raise what you saw and what the person is asking for. The review belongs to them. You bring the evidence of their week. You do not rewrite their goals in the staff room.",
  ],
  "PS-03|The five types": [
    "There are five regulated restrictive practices. Seclusion confines someone alone so they cannot leave. Chemical restraint is medication used to control behaviour rather than to treat a diagnosed condition, and some PRN is in that category. Mechanical restraint is a device used to restrict movement in order to control behaviour. Physical restraint is using your body to restrict their movement. Environmental restraint restricts access to a place or a thing: a locked door, fridge, cupboard, or gate, when the purpose is control.",
    "The same lock, medicine, or hold can be ordinary care in one situation and a restrictive practice in another. The difference is the purpose, and whether the person can freely stop it. Learn to see the five in daily routines, not only in a crisis.",
  ],
  "PS-03|Hidden restrictive practices": [
    "A rule that 'applies to everyone' can still restrict the person who cannot leave, cannot open the fridge, or cannot choose. Eating at five can be a shared routine. Locking the cupboard so one person cannot eat is environmental restraint when they cannot consent and cannot get to their own food.",
    "Practices hide behind 'for their safety' and behind withholding a possession, an outing, or a cigarette until someone complies. If the person cannot freely end it, question it. A kind motive does not change what it is.",
  ],
  "PS-03|Authorisation": [
    "A regulated restrictive practice has to be in a behaviour support plan and authorised under the state or territory process. Agreeing on shift does not authorise it. The plan should show it is the least restrictive option, used for the shortest time, and watched.",
    "Punishment, convenience, and filling a short roster are never reasons. If the plan is silent, you do not do it. Use the proactive strategies, and report any pressure to do something the plan does not allow.",
  ],
  "PS-03|Reporting": [
    "Authorised use is reported monthly so people can see whether the practice is reducing. Record the time, how long, and what you tried first. 'Used the plan' is not a report.",
    "Unauthorised use is a reportable incident, notified within five business days. Tell your provider as soon as you know. Hiding it, or delaying it so the numbers look better, breaks the Code.",
  ],
  "PS-03|Reduction": [
    "The plan should say how the practice will fade: which skill or change will make it unnecessary. If the same restraint has been used for a year and nothing else has changed, say so.",
    "Use the alternatives every shift, not only when you remember. Speak up when a practice is no longer needed, when it is used outside its conditions, or when a less restrictive option is being ignored.",
  ],
  "PS-04|Access": [
    "People are offered the service without discrimination. Explain what you do, and what you do not do, in language they can use. If there is a waitlist, say how it works and what happens while they wait.",
    "If you decline, give the reason, in writing if they want it, and refer on where you can. Refusing because someone's disability looks inconvenient is discrimination, whatever word you use for it.",
  ],
  "PS-04|Service agreements": [
    "The agreement says what you will do, what it costs, how it can change or end, and how to complain. It is written so the person can understand it. Easy Read is part of that when they need it.",
    "They consent, they sign if they are able, and they keep a copy. A signature rushed so billing can start is not consent. Review the agreement when the support changes. An old document that no longer matches the week is not an agreement.",
  ],
  "PS-04|Pricing and changes": [
    "Charge within the price limits that apply to that support. Do not invent a fee, and do not describe a support as a more expensive one than it was. Explain cancellation rules before they are used: how much notice, and what is charged.",
    "When price, day, or worker arrangements change, tell the person in advance in a way they understand, and update the agreement if the change matters. A text on the morning is not notice.",
  ],
  "PS-04|Transitions": [
    "An exit or a transfer is planned so the person knows what is happening, agrees as far as they can, and does not lose essential support in the gap. Tell them the last day, what happens to their information, and who they can call.",
    "Share information with the next provider only with consent, and share what the next workers need to keep them safe. Until the end date, medication, meals, and personal care continue. A sour relationship is not a reason to stop.",
  ],
  "PS-05|The SIL standards": [
    "Supported independent living is the support to live in a home, not the building. It sits inside registration because people were harmed in houses where nobody outside was responsible for the standard of their life.",
    "You need the parts that change a shift: whose plan you follow, which skills you must have before a task, and which 'house rules' are actually rights restrictions. Follow each person's plan. Do not invent a blanket rule to make the house easier to run.",
  ],
  "PS-05|Individual within shared": [
    "Each person has their own plan, preferences, and routines. The house does not get one lifestyle because the roster prefers it. Privacy means their bedroom, conversations that are not held about them in the kitchen, and personal care out of sight of housemates.",
    "A rule about shared noise can be fair. A rule that stops visitors, locks one person's food, or sets a shower time for staff convenience is not a house rule. It may be a restrictive practice, and it is a rights issue either way.",
  ],
  "PS-05|House-level risk": [
    "People who live together can harm each other. Conflict and abuse between residents are incidents. Watch for fear, injuries, missing belongings, and one person controlling another's food, money, or room.",
    "Locking everyone's cupboards, or using everyone's PRN to keep the house quiet, is not individual support. Money is recorded per person. On overnight shifts you still respond if someone is unsafe. Waiting until morning because you were the sleepover is not a response.",
  ],
  "PS-05|Records": [
    "A useful record shows each person's week: what they did, what they chose, what went wrong, and what changed. Handover is part of that. A code word or a joke on the whiteboard is not a record of a person.",
    "House meetings count when each person can take part, including people who do not use speech. What is written should be their view, not only what was convenient for staff. If the record cannot show how this person lives, the risks are invisible too.",
  ],
  "SE-01|The principles": [
    "The National Principles for Child Safe Organisations say children are safe when the organisation listens to them, staff are suitable and trained, risks are known, and complaints are acted on. States add their own child safe standards. You follow the ones that apply where you work.",
    "A Working With Children Check is required where the law says so. A clearance is not proof someone is safe. It is one screen. You still watch practice, and you still report concerns. A check does not replace your judgement on a shift.",
  ],
  "SE-01|Mandatory reporting": [
    "Mandatory reporting laws differ by state. Know whether your role must report, what harm must be reported, and to which child protection agency. 'I told my manager' may not complete your personal duty if you are a mandated reporter.",
    "Reportable conduct schemes cover certain allegations about workers, including sexual offences, sexual misconduct, and serious physical harm. You report allegations. You do not decide they are unlikely and keep them in the house. If you are unsure whether it meets the line, report.",
  ],
  "SE-01|Grooming and boundaries": [
    "Grooming is a pattern that builds trust in order to abuse: gifts, secrets, special treatment, time alone, and slowly crossing physical boundaries. One kind act is not grooming. Secrecy plus special access is the warning.",
    "One-to-one settings — cars, bedrooms, personal care, overnight — are where this happens. Follow the rules about who is present, doors, and recording. Do not agree to 'don't tell your mum' or 'this is our secret', and do not let a colleague create that pattern.",
  ],
  "SE-01|Disclosures": [
    "If a child tells you something, listen. Use their words. Do not ask a string of questions that puts ideas in their mouth, and do not make them repeat it for everyone on shift. Say they did the right thing by telling you, and that you will get help. Do not promise to keep it secret.",
    "Write down what they said, as soon as you can, as close to their words as you can. Then report through the child protection path and your provider. Do not tell the person they accused, and do not investigate it yourself.",
  ],
  "SE-01|Safe practice": [
    "Personal care of children is planned: who does it, how privacy is kept, and how the child is involved in the way that fits their age. Explain what you are doing. Stop if they are distressed and get guidance. Do not decide that speed matters more than their comfort.",
    "Transport and overnight support need the safeguards in the plan: who else knows the trip, how contact is made, where they sleep. Online and phone contact with a child happens only through the channels the provider has agreed. A private social media friendship with a child you support is a boundary breach.",
  ],
  "SE-02|Why it matters": [
    "Coughs and sneezes spread viruses in droplets, and some infections also travel in smaller aerosols that hang in the air. In a car, a bathroom, or a close personal-care task, you are in that space with the person.",
    "Many people you support are more likely to become seriously unwell from a respiratory infection. Your habits protect them. Staying home when you are sick is part of the same protection.",
  ],
  "SE-02|Cover, catch, clean": [
    "Cover a cough or sneeze with a tissue, or with the inside of your elbow if you have no tissue. Catch it. Do not use an open hand you then put on a bench, a hoist, or someone's arm.",
    "The tissue goes in a bin, then you clean your hands. If you are symptomatic and still in a setting where you should not be, a mask reduces what you spread, but the right step is usually to be off the shift. Offer the person the same tools: tissues, a bin, and help to clean their hands if they want it.",
  ],
  "SE-02|Supporting participants": [
    "Some people need a prompt to cover a cough, or a tissue put in their hand. Teach it the way you teach any other skill: show, prompt, and do not shame them when they cannot do it yet.",
    "People who cannot cover a cough because of disability still need protection around them. You step back if you can, you clean surfaces, you wear what the situation requires, and you do not blame them for spreading something they cannot physically stop.",
  ],
  "SE-03|Spread and risk": [
    "Respiratory viruses spread in droplets from coughs and sneezes, in smaller aerosols in shared air, and from surfaces people then touch. A home, a van, and a shared house are all places this happens.",
    "Some participants are at higher risk of severe illness: older people, people with respiratory or immune conditions, and people who cannot avoid close contact because they need personal care. Their plan, not a general rule of thumb, tells you what extra precautions they need.",
  ],
  "SE-03|Precautions": [
    "Standard precautions stay in place: clean hands, respiratory hygiene, the right protective equipment, and safe waste. Transmission-based precautions add masks, eye protection, or gowns when the illness or the task requires them. Follow the current instruction for that person and that illness. Do not invent a lower standard because you are 'used to them'.",
    "Ventilation reduces what hangs in the air. Open a window when you can, and do not sit in a closed car with someone who is coughing if the plan says otherwise. Masks are worn properly, changed when wet, and thrown away. They are not worn under the chin.",
  ],
  "SE-03|Outbreak procedures": [
    "An outbreak is more than one person unwell in a way that suggests the same infection is spreading, in a house or a team. You notice it. You do not decide it is 'just a cold going around' and keep rotating staff through the house.",
    "Isolation and cohorting mean unwell people are supported with as little mixing as possible, and staff movement is limited if that is the instruction. Notify the person your process names, and follow public health directions that apply. Those directions outrank a preference to keep the usual roster.",
  ],
  "SE-03|Supporting unwell participants": [
    "Watch the symptoms the plan names: breathing, fever, fluid intake, alertness, and the signs that mean you escalate. Write them down so the next shift can see a change, not a vague 'seems a bit off'.",
    "Escalate when breathing is hard, when they are drowsy, when they cannot keep fluids down, or when the plan says to. Isolation does not mean they lose support. Meals, medication, personal care, and company in a way that is safe still happen. A closed door is not a care plan.",
  ],
  "SE-04|Foodborne illness": [
    "Foodborne illness comes from bacteria, viruses, and toxins in food that was undercooked, left out, or contaminated. The person may not be sick until hours later, so the cause is easy to miss.",
    "Some people become dangerously dehydrated or unwell from a bout that someone else would shake off. Follow their plan, and do not serve food you would not trust yourself. When in doubt, throw it out.",
  ],
  "SE-04|Temperature and time": [
    "Bacteria grow fastest between 5 and 60 degrees. That is the danger zone. Hot food stays hot, cold food stays cold, and food does not sit on a bench while you do another task.",
    "Cool leftovers quickly, reheat them until they are steaming hot, and respect use-by dates. A sniff is not a test. If the person has a texture-modified meal, the safety rules still apply. You do not leave a thickened drink in a warm room all afternoon.",
  ],
  "SE-04|Contamination and allergens": [
    "Keep raw meat away from food that will not be cooked again. Use separate boards or wash them properly between. Clean your hands after raw food, after the toilet, and before you eat or help someone eat.",
    "Allergens kill. Read the plan, read labels, and do not swap a product because it looks similar. An anaphylaxis plan tells you the signs and how the adrenaline device is used. Know where the device is before the meal, not during the reaction.",
  ],
  "SE-04|In practice": [
    "Participant kitchens are not commercial kitchens, and they are still places people get sick. Look at the fridge, the dates, and the cloth that wipes everything. Shopping and transport: cold food stays cold on the way home. Do not leave it in a hot car while you do another errand.",
    "Clean as you go. A bench used for raw chicken is not then used for a sandwich. If you support someone to cook, build the safe step into the task — washing hands, checking the date — rather than doing it silently and calling it their independence.",
  ],
  "SE-05|Chain of infection": [
    "Infection needs a chain: a germ, a place it lives, a way out, a way it travels, a way in, and a person it can infect. Break any link and the infection does not travel. You break it with clean hands, with covering coughs, with handling waste safely, with cleaning, and by not working when you are infectious.",
    "The chain is the same in a home, a van, and a centre. You do not need to know the name of the germ to interrupt it.",
  ],
  "SE-05|Standard precautions": [
    "Standard precautions are used with every person, whether or not they have a known infection. Hand hygiene. Protective equipment matched to the task. Respiratory hygiene. Safe sharps and waste. Basic aseptic technique when you are doing something that must stay clean, such as a wound or a catheter, as you have been taught.",
    "You do not scale these down because you know the person, or because the shift is short. The precautions are the support.",
  ],
  "SE-05|Hand hygiene": [
    "The five moments are: before touching a person, before a clean task, after body fluid, after touching the person, and after touching their surroundings. These are the times that protect them, not a wash at the start and end of the shift only.",
    "Use alcohol rub when hands are not visibly dirty. Use soap and water when they are dirty, after the toilet, and after body fluids that rub does not handle well. Gloves are not a substitute. You clean your hands when the gloves come off.",
  ],
  "SE-05|PPE": [
    "Gloves, gowns or aprons, masks, and eye protection each do a different job. Choose what the task needs. Gloves for fluids and personal care. A gown if your clothes may be splashed. A mask and eye protection if fluid may hit your face.",
    "Put on and take off in the order you were taught, so you do not contaminate your face on the way out. Equipment fits, and it is single use unless it is specifically reusable and you know how it is cleaned. A mask under the chin is not worn.",
  ],
  "SE-05|Cleaning": [
    "Cleaning removes dirt. Disinfecting kills germs that cleaning leaves behind. You clean first, then disinfect when the procedure says to, and you leave the product for the contact time on the label.",
    "Shared equipment — hoists, wheelchairs, blood pressure cuffs, vehicles — is cleaned between people. Laundry soiled with body fluid is bagged where it is, not shaken through the house, and washed as the provider directs.",
  ],
  "SE-05|Outbreaks": [
    "Signs of an outbreak are more than one person unwell with a similar illness, in a house or a team, close together in time. New coughs, vomiting, or diarrhoea in several people is a reason to tell someone the same day.",
    "Notification follows your process and any public health instruction. Transmission-based precautions are the extra steps for that illness: the mask, the gown, the limit on who comes and goes. You do not decide the outbreak is over because you are tired of the precautions.",
  ],
  "SE-06|Before use": [
    "Before you use a hoist or a sling, check the service tag and the load limit. Out of date or overloaded equipment is how people get dropped. The sling has to be the right type, the right size, and the loops the plan names. A sling from another house is not 'close enough'.",
    "The person's manual handling plan says which equipment, how many workers, and what they can do themselves. Read it. If the equipment does not match the plan, you do not invent a lift while you wait.",
  ],
  "SE-06|Hoists": [
    "A full-body hoist moves someone who cannot stand safely. You explain each step, fit the sling as taught, keep them involved, and move smoothly. Brakes and the path are checked before you raise them.",
    "A stand hoist is only for a person the plan says can bear weight and follow the steps. Two-person tasks stay two-person. If the second worker is not there, the task waits or you get help. You do not do a two-person hoist alone because the shift is short.",
  ],
  "SE-06|Slide sheets and boards": [
    "A slide sheet reduces friction so you can reposition someone up or across the bed without dragging their skin or your back. The sheet is under them properly, you have the number of workers the plan says, and you agree the count before you move.",
    "A transfer board bridges two surfaces at a similar height for a person who can assist. It is not a slide from a high bed to a low chair. If the gap, the height, or the person's ability does not match what you were taught, stop.",
  ],
  "SE-06|Faults": [
    "Equipment that is broken, out of service date, or behaving oddly is tagged out so the next person does not use it. Report it the same shift. A hoist that made a new noise is not 'probably fine'.",
    "Never improvise a lift with a sheet, a belt, or two people under the arms because the equipment failed. Wait, get the right equipment, or get a clinical decision. An improvised lift is how workers and participants are injured.",
  ],
  "SE-07|Assess first": [
    "Read the manual handling plan, then look at the person today. Pain, fatigue, new shoes or no shoes, a wet floor, or a day when they are much less steady changes the support. Yesterday's easy walk can be today's fall.",
    "Ask them how they are and watch them sit before you ask them to stand. If the plan and the person in front of you disagree, the safer assessment wins, and you record why.",
  ],
  "SE-07|Sit-to-stand and walking": [
    "Set their feet, give them time, and cue in the words the plan uses. Do not pull on their arms. Stand close, on their weaker side if that is the instruction, and let them do the work they can do.",
    "Belts and walking aids are used only as assessed. A belt is not a handle to haul someone up. Match their pace. Rushing the walk to stay on time is how falls happen.",
  ],
  "SE-07|Wheelchairs": [
    "Brakes on before a transfer. Footplates managed so they are not a trip hazard and so their feet are supported when you move. Tilt and recline only as the seating plan allows. A wheelchair is prescribed. Cushions and settings are not yours to 'improve'.",
    "Kerbs and ramps are done the way you were taught, with the person told before you tip or turn. In vehicles, the chair and the person are secured as the vehicle requires. Their postural belt is not the vehicle restraint.",
  ],
  "SE-07|Falls in progress": [
    "If a person is falling, do not catch their weight. Guide them down if you can do that without injuring yourself, and protect their head. Catching a falling adult injures your back and can injure them.",
    "Afterwards, assess before anyone moves them. Head strike, pain, blood-thinning medication, and confusion mean you get help rather than getting them up to 'see'. Getting up, when it is safe, follows the plan. Then record the fall.",
  ],
  "SE-08|Set-up": [
    "Set the bed, the commode, or the shower chair at a height that lets you work without bending and twisting. Bring what you need before you start so you are not leaving them half-supported while you look for a towel.",
    "Awkward postures — twisting while holding, reaching across a bed, kneeling on a wet floor — are how cumulative injury happens. Change the setup. Do not 'just manage' a bad position because it is quicker.",
  ],
  "SE-08|In bed": [
    "Rolling and moving someone up the bed uses the sheet or the technique in the plan, with the number of people it names. You explain, you agree a count, and you avoid dragging skin.",
    "If the plan says two people, one person does not do it 'just this once'. Get the second person or change the task. A single-worker drag is an injury to both of you.",
  ],
  "SE-08|Bathroom": [
    "Wet floors, soap, and small rooms make bathrooms the place people fall. Dry the floor, use the non-slip equipment, and keep the path clear before they stand.",
    "Shower chair and commode transfers follow the plan: brakes, the right height, the right number of workers. You do not transfer onto a wet, braked-off chair because the water is getting cold.",
  ],
  "SE-08|Dignity": [
    "Explain what you are about to do, ask, and cover the parts of the body you are not washing. Shut the door. The fact that you have done this a hundred times does not make it ordinary for them today.",
    "Work at their pace. Being cold, exposed, and rushed is a harm even when the task is 'done properly'. If they want to do a part themselves, wait for that part.",
  ],
  "SE-09|Injury": [
    "Your spine is injured by bending, twisting, and lifting at the same time, and by doing that in small amounts all year. Cumulative strain does not feel like an accident. It feels like a back that is a bit worse every month, until a small task finishes it.",
    "Care work has high rates of musculoskeletal injury. The techniques exist because workers were hurt, and because participants were dropped. Using them is part of being able to keep supporting people.",
  ],
  "SE-09|Principles": [
    "Plan the move before you start. Assess the load, the environment, and the person today. Communicate what will happen, and position your feet so you can move without twisting.",
    "Move with your legs and your whole body, not by hinging your back and hauling. If you cannot get close, if you have to twist, or if the person cannot do the part the plan assumes, stop and reset. Force is not a technique.",
  ],
  "SE-09|Plans and policies": [
    "The manual handling plan is specific to the person: which transfer, which aid, how many workers. A no-lift approach means you do not manually lift someone's full weight. You use equipment.",
    "Two-person tasks are planned that way because one person cannot do them safely. The policy applies on a Sunday night as much as on a training day. If you cannot meet it, you delay the task and call, rather than quietly lifting.",
  ],
  "SE-09|Refusing unsafe tasks": [
    "You have the right to stop a task you reasonably believe will injure you or the person. Stopping is a safety action. Say what is unsafe and what you need: the hoist, the second person, a different method.",
    "Report it so the task is not left for the next worker to attempt the same way. Alternatives might be equipment, a changed time, or a clinical review of the plan. There is no alternative called 'we always just lift'.",
  ],
  "SE-10|Before transfer": [
    "Park so the door opens fully and the person is not stepping into traffic. Look at the kerb, the seat height, and the slope. A transfer that worked in a driveway may not work on a camber.",
    "Use the aids the plan names: a swivel cushion, a board, a handle. Do not use a worker's body as the aid because the cushion was left at home. Go back for it, or change the plan for today with help.",
  ],
  "SE-10|Car transfers": [
    "The usual pattern is sit first, then bring the legs in, and the reverse to get out. You support and guide. You do not lift a person into a seat by their arms or under their shoulders.",
    "If they cannot do the movement the plan describes, stop. Forcing a transfer into a low car seat is a common way to injure a shoulder, yours or theirs. A different vehicle or a different method is the answer.",
  ],
  "SE-10|WAVs": [
    "Wheelchair accessible vehicles use ramps or hoists. Use them as you were shown, with the chair facing the way the procedure says, and brakes on at the right moments.",
    "The wheelchair is tied down at the four points designed for it. The person wears the vehicle's occupant restraint. The wheelchair's own postural belt does not hold them in a crash. If you cannot secure both, you do not drive.",
  ],
  "SE-10|In transit": [
    "Know what you will do if the person has a seizure, becomes distressed, or is motion sick. Where you can safely stop, who you call, and what the plan says about positioning. You do not pull over in a live lane to fix a problem you could have planned.",
    "Distress in a moving vehicle is a reason to stop safely and use the strategies, not a reason to drive faster to get it over with. Their safety and yours include arriving.",
  ],
  "SE-11|Roles and authority": [
    "There is a difference between prompting someone to take their own medication, assisting them, and administering it. Your role, your training, and the state rules decide which of those you may do. The medication authority or treatment sheet is the instruction. Your memory is not.",
    "This course is the theory. Being signed off as competent to assist or administer is separate. Until you have that sign-off for this task, you do not do it. Scope is not expanded because the usual worker is away.",
  ],
  "SE-11|The rights": [
    "The rights of medication support are the checks that stop the wrong thing happening: the right person, medicine, dose, time, route, and the right to refuse, plus the documentation and the reason you are giving it. Whatever list of eight your provider uses, you do every check, every time.",
    "Check against the chart, not against what you gave yesterday. Identify the person the way the procedure says, even if you know them. Similar names and webster packs are how errors happen between people who live together.",
  ],
  "SE-11|Systems": [
    "Dose administration aids, such as blister packs, are prepared by a pharmacist. You do not add, remove, or swap tablets because a pack looks wrong. You stop and check.",
    "Sign the chart at the time you give the medicine, not at the end of the shift from memory. A change from a prescriber is written on the chart through the proper process. A family member's verbal instruction, or a text, is not a new order.",
  ],
  "SE-11|Storage": [
    "Medication is stored as the procedure says, usually locked, away from heat, and separate from other people's medicines. Refrigerated items stay in the fridge that is used for them, at the right temperature, not in a door that swings warm all day.",
    "Schedule 8 medicines have extra recording. You follow that register exactly. Disposal of unwanted medicine goes back to a pharmacy or the stream your provider uses. It does not go in the kitchen bin or down the sink.",
  ],
  "SE-11|When things go wrong": [
    "Errors and near misses are reported the same shift. A missed dose, a double dose, or the wrong person. Say so immediately. Waiting to see if they 'seem fine' wastes the window for advice.",
    "A person may refuse medication. That can be an informed choice. You do not hide it in food, and you do not coerce them. You record the refusal and tell the person the plan names. An adverse reaction or anaphylaxis is an emergency: follow the action plan, call for help, and use the adrenaline device if that is what the plan says and you are trained to do it.",
  ],
  "SE-11|Chemical restraint": [
    "A medicine given to control behaviour, rather than to treat the condition it was prescribed for, can be a chemical restraint. PRN used because someone is distressed, loud, or 'difficult' is the common case. The behaviour support plan says whether that medicine is authorised as a restrictive practice and what must be tried first.",
    "You do not give it early, give an extra dose, or give it because the house is short-staffed. If it is not authorised, you do not give it for behaviour. You report the request. Authorised use is recorded so it can be reviewed and reduced.",
  ],
  "SE-12|Waste streams": [
    "Waste is separated so the right thing is treated safely. General waste, recycling, clinical waste, sharps, pharmaceutical waste, and hazardous waste are not the same bin. Colour and labelling tell you which is which in that house. Learn them before you need them.",
    "If you do not know, ask. Putting clinical waste in recycling, or tablets in the household bin, creates a risk for the person who handles it next.",
  ],
  "SE-12|Handling": [
    "Wear the protective equipment the waste requires. Gloves at a minimum for anything soiled. Hold bags away from your body. Do not compress a bag with your hands to make it fit.",
    "Seal bags before you carry them. Store waste where the procedure says, out of kitchens and out of reach, until it is collected. A tied bag in the bathroom for three days is not storage. It is a hazard.",
  ],
  "SE-12|Special cases": [
    "Continence aids in a private home may go in general waste if that is the local rule and they are bagged so they do not leak. In other settings they are clinical waste. Follow the rule for that place, not the rule from your last house.",
    "Unused medication goes back to a pharmacy. Sharps go in a sharps container, not in a bag of continence aids. A container that is full past the line is replaced, not pressed down.",
  ],
  "SE-13|Selecting": [
    "Choose protective equipment from the risk in front of you, not from habit. Gloves when you will touch fluid or broken skin. A gown or apron when your clothes may be contaminated. A mask when there is a respiratory risk or splash to the mouth. Eye protection when fluid may hit your face. A respirator when the instruction is a respirator, not a surgical mask.",
    "More equipment is not always safer. Gloves worn for the entire shift, touching door handles and then a sandwich, spread contamination. Take them off between tasks.",
  ],
  "SE-13|Donning and doffing": [
    "Put equipment on in the order you were taught, so the parts that must stay clean stay clean. Take it off in the order that keeps contaminated surfaces away from your face and clothes. The doffing is where people infect themselves.",
    "Common errors: touching the outside of a glove with a bare hand, pulling a mask down and then back up, and wiping your face with a gloved hand. If you are unsure of the order, stop and follow the poster or ask. Guessing is how the germ gets to your mouth.",
  ],
  "SE-13|Masks": [
    "A surgical mask is a barrier for droplets. A P2 or N95 respirator filters smaller particles and has to fit your face. They are not interchangeable. Use the one the situation requires.",
    "A fit check is the breath in and out that shows air is not leaking around a respirator. Facial hair and a poor size defeat it. Masks are single use. You do not hang one on a mirror for tomorrow, and you do not wear it under your chin between tasks.",
  ],
  "SE-14|Dysphagia": [
    "Dysphagia means difficulty swallowing. Food or drink can go into the airway instead of the stomach. People at higher risk include those with neurological disability, a history of choking, or coughing during meals. The plan will say if this person is one of them.",
    "Signs are coughing during or after a meal, a wet or gurgly voice, throat clearing, food left in the mouth, and repeated chest infections. Silent aspiration has few signs. That is why you follow the plan even when the meal 'looks fine'.",
  ],
  "SE-14|Mealtime plans": [
    "A mealtime management plan is written by a qualified practitioner, often a speech pathologist, with the person. It says texture, thickness of fluids, position, pace, equipment, and what to do if they choke. You do not write one yourself from experience.",
    "Follow it every meal, including snacks and cups of tea. Ask for a review when swallowing changes: more coughing, a new chest infection, weight loss, or refusal. A plan from two years ago may be dangerous if their swallow has changed.",
  ],
  "SE-14|IDDSI": [
    "IDDSI levels describe food texture and drink thickness so a kitchen and a support worker mean the same thing. The plan names the level. 'Soft' and 'a bit thick' are not levels.",
    "Test the texture the way you were taught for that level, and thicken fluids to the level on the plan, using the product named. Too thin can go into the airway. Too thick can be unsafe as well. Do not guess with a random thickener or a fork test you invented.",
  ],
  "SE-14|Safe support": [
    "The person sits upright, as the plan describes, for the meal and for the time afterwards it specifies. Feeding someone who is reclined is how aspiration happens. Pace the meal. Watch fatigue. A tired swallow is a less safe swallow.",
    "Reduce distractions if the plan says they need to focus. Oral care matters because leftover food and poor mouth care lead to chest infections. Support teeth and mouth cleaning as part of the mealtime, not as an optional extra.",
  ],
  "SE-14|Emergencies": [
    "Know the choking response you were taught: encourage coughing if they can cough, and use the emergency steps if they cannot breathe, cough, or speak. Call 000 when the plan says to, and whenever they cannot clear the airway.",
    "After a choking event, even if they seem fine, follow the plan for monitoring and report it. A near miss at lunch is how the plan gets reviewed before dinner.",
  ],
  "SE-15|Five moments": [
    "Clean your hands before you touch a participant, so you do not bring in what was on the last surface. Clean them before a clean or aseptic task, such as a wound or a catheter. Clean them after body fluid exposure, even if you wore gloves.",
    "Clean them after touching the person, and after touching their surroundings: the bed rail, the wheelchair, the bench. The five moments are the practice. A wash when you arrive and when you leave misses the moments that matter.",
  ],
  "SE-15|Technique": [
    "Soap and water takes 40 to 60 seconds, all surfaces, including thumbs and fingertips, then dry with a clean towel. Alcohol rub takes 20 to 30 seconds, until the hands are dry. Wet hands are not finished hands.",
    "Soap and water is required when hands are visibly dirty, after the toilet, and when the procedure says rub is not enough. If the product is not there, get it. Skipping because the bottle is empty is an infection decision.",
  ],
  "SE-15|Barriers": [
    "Broken skin and dermatitis make hand hygiene painful, so people skip it. Report skin problems and use the moisturiser you are allowed to use. Do not stop cleaning your hands.",
    "Long nails, chipped polish, and rings trap germs. Keep nails short and plain at work. Gloves are a barrier for the task, not a replacement for clean hands, and they come off before you touch your face, a pen, or the next person.",
  ],
  "SE-16|Duties": [
    "A PCBU is the business that must keep workers and others safe, so far as reasonably practicable. Officers, including directors, must exercise due diligence: understand the risks, make sure resources and processes exist, and make sure those processes are used. Workers must take care and follow the reasonable instruction.",
    "Due diligence is active. Asking for the incident reports, asking whether manual handling equipment is in date, and acting when it is not. Penalties apply when duties are breached, including after someone is hurt. The legal duty and the duty to the person are the same work.",
  ],
  "SE-16|Hazards in disability support": [
    "The hazards that hurt workers in this sector are manual handling, occupational violence, psychosocial hazards such as bullying and traumatic shifts, and working alone in houses and cars. They are predictable. A leader who is surprised by them has not looked.",
    "Controls have to work in other people's homes, not only in a centre with a store room. That means equipment in the house, a way to get help at night, and rosters that do not leave one worker to do a two-person task.",
  ],
  "SE-16|Systems": [
    "Consultation means workers, including health and safety representatives, are asked about the risks before you decide. The people doing the transfer know whether the hoist is usable. Hazard reporting has to be easy, and it has to lead to a fix, or people stop reporting.",
    "Investigate incidents for the cause, not only the injury. Training is part of the system: people are taught the task before they are rostered to it, and the training matches the equipment in that house.",
  ],
  "SE-16|Notifiable incidents": [
    "Some workplace incidents must be notified to the work health and safety regulator: a death, a serious injury or illness, and a dangerous incident, as the law defines them. Know the list your organisation uses, and know who makes the call. You do not wait for a convenient office hour if the rule is immediate.",
    "Preserve the site when the law requires it, so far as you can without putting anyone in further danger. Do not tidy a scene that has to be seen. Timeframes are short. A late notification is a second failure.",
  ],
  "SE-16|Injury management": [
    "Workers compensation is how an injured worker gets medical care and income support. Leaders do not talk people out of reporting an injury. Early reporting is how recovery starts.",
    "Return to work is planned, suitable, and does not put them back on the task that injured them before they are fit. A 'light' shift that is actually the same two-person hoist is not a return-to-work plan.",
  ],
  "SE-17|Duties": [
    "You take reasonable care of your own health and safety, and of others who are affected by what you do. That includes the participant. You follow the reasonable safety instruction, and you cooperate with the procedures.",
    "You may refuse work you reasonably believe is unsafe, and you are protected from being punished for that. Refusing is saying what is unsafe and asking for it to be fixed. It is not walking off and leaving someone without essential support and without telling anyone.",
  ],
  "SE-17|Hazards": [
    "Manual handling, slips, trips, and falls are the everyday injuries. Chemicals and hot surfaces show up in bathrooms and kitchens: bleach mixed with other cleaners, a kettle, a heater. Do not mix chemicals, and do not leave hot water where someone who cannot move away will be burned.",
    "Dogs, smoking, and hoarding are hazards in people's homes. You do not ignore a dog that has bitten, or a path you cannot walk. Driving is a work task when you drive for support. Fatigue, a phone, and an unsecured wheelchair are driving hazards. Deal with them before you move.",
  ],
  "SE-17|Working alone": [
    "Know the check-in: who expects to hear from you, and when they will come looking. Use it. A system you skip on the quiet shifts is a system that fails on the bad one.",
    "Plan how you would leave. Keys, a clear path, a phone that is charged. A duress alarm or a code word only works if someone is listening and you have practised what happens next. If the house is not safe to enter, you do not enter alone to be polite.",
  ],
  "SE-17|Occupational violence": [
    "Warning signs are a rising voice, threats, a person cornering you, or a change from their usual distress into something aimed at you. Use the de-escalation in the plan: space, calm voice, a smaller demand. Do not match the volume, and do not block the only exit.",
    "You may leave when you are at risk, once the participant is as safe as you can make them and you have called for help. Then report it. Violence is not 'part of the job' that you absorb. Support after it is part of the response, including for you.",
  ],
  "SE-17|Reporting": [
    "Report hazards when you see them, not after someone is hurt. A loose rail, a hoist out of date, a dog, a roster that leaves you alone for a two-person task. Near misses are reports. The sling that slipped and caught is a near miss.",
    "Ask what happens next if you do not hear back. Reporting into silence teaches people to stop. If nothing changes, tell the next person up. The hazard remains until someone acts.",
  ],
  "SE-18|Rules": [
    "You handle a participant's money and property only as they, or their proper authority, have agreed, and only for the purpose agreed. You do not become their unofficial banker.",
    "You do not know their PIN, you do not store their card in your wallet, and you do not use their online banking. Gifts and borrowing either way are boundary breaches. 'They insisted' does not make it theirs to give if the relationship is one of dependence.",
  ],
  "SE-18|Recording": [
    "Every amount has a receipt or a record: what was spent, when, and what it was for. Cash is logged and reconciled. The person's money and yours never share a pocket.",
    "Two-person checks, where the procedure uses them, exist so one worker cannot move money unseen. Do them. A float that only one person ever counts is a risk to the participant and to you.",
  ],
  "SE-18|Financial abuse": [
    "Signs include missing money, a person suddenly unable to pay for things they used to buy, a new 'friend' who takes them to the bank, or a family member who speaks for them about every purchase while they look afraid.",
    "Family and informal supports are sometimes the source, not the protection. You do not need proof. You record what you saw and report it through the safeguarding path. Do not confront the person you suspect, and do not warn them that you are going to report.",
  ],
  "SE-18|Supporting control": [
    "Where the plan is to build their control of money, support the step they can do: choosing the item, handing over the card, checking the change. Do not take over because it is faster, and do not leave them open to exploitation to prove they are independent.",
    "Accessible banking might be pictures, a small amount of cash, or a support person they chose. Independence is the goal. It includes the right to make a purchase you think is unwise, with the same dignity-of-risk conversation you would have about any other choice.",
  ],
  "AS-01|Duties": [
    "Office, reception, finance, and rostering work is still work. You take reasonable care of yourself and of anyone affected by what you do, including participants whose plans and rosters you handle. You report hazards rather than working around them.",
    "Consultation means you are asked about changes that affect safety, and you speak up. A roster you build that leaves one worker to do a two-person lift is a safety decision, even though you made it at a desk.",
  ],
  "AS-01|Workstations": [
    "Set the chair so your feet are supported and your shoulders are relaxed. The screen is at about eye level, the keyboard close enough that you are not reaching. Take breaks from sitting. Pain that arrives every afternoon is information, not a badge of effort.",
    "Working from home does not remove the duty. You still need a setup that does not injure you, and a way to raise a hazard. A laptop on a couch for a full day is a hazard you report, not a perk you endure.",
  ],
  "AS-01|Office hazards": [
    "Cables across walkways, boxes in front of exits, and overfilled filing cabinets injure people in offices. Keep exits clear. Close drawers. Do not stand on a chair to reach a shelf.",
    "Kitchens have knives, boiling water, and wet floors. Clean spills when they happen. Electrical hazards are damaged cords, overloaded boards, and equipment that smells hot. Take it out of use and report it. Do not tape a cord and keep going.",
  ],
  "AS-01|Psychosocial": [
    "Reception and phones are where aggressive callers and visitors land. You are allowed to end a call that is abusive, to get a colleague, and to not be the person who absorbs it alone. The organisation needs a way to do that, and you need to use it.",
    "Workload that cannot be done in the hours you have is a psychosocial hazard. Say what is not getting done and what the risk is, especially if the undone work is screening, incidents, or rosters. Support includes supervision and the employee assistance program. Using them is ordinary.",
  ],
  "AS-01|Emergencies": [
    "Know the evacuation route, the assembly point, and who needs help to get out, including any participant or visitor on site. Walk it before the alarm, not during it.",
    "Know who the first aid officers are and where the kit is. After an emergency, report what happened and what made it harder: a blocked exit, a list of people that was wrong, an alarm nobody could hear. That is how the next one goes better.",
  ],
  "AS-02|Threats": [
    "Phishing and smishing are fake emails and texts that want a password, a payment, or a click. Invoice and payroll fraud asks you to change bank details. A manager who 'urgently' wants gift cards or a quiet transfer is a classic impersonation. Ransomware locks systems and often starts with one click.",
    "Slow down when a message is urgent, secret, or slightly wrong. Check through a second channel you already trust, not through the number or link in the message. Participant information and worker identity documents are what these attacks are after.",
  ],
  "AS-02|Access": [
    "Use a long passphrase you do not reuse. Multi-factor authentication stops a stolen password being enough. Turn it on where you are told to, and do not approve a prompt you were not expecting.",
    "Never share a login, including with a colleague who is 'just helping', and including a password on a sticky note. Lock the screen when you stand up. An unlocked computer in a shared office is an open file about participants.",
  ],
  "AS-02|Devices and data": [
    "Phones and laptops in cars and homes get stolen. They are not left on a seat, and they are locked. Participant information does not sit in a personal photo library.",
    "Public Wi-Fi is not where you open participant records. Unknown USB drives are not plugged in. Use the apps the provider has approved. A convenient free app is a place their information can go that you cannot get back.",
  ],
  "AS-02|Sharing": [
    "Email and messaging can send a whole file to the wrong place in one click. Check the address. Beware autocomplete. Do not put health information in a chat that was set up for roster swaps.",
    "Sending to the wrong recipient is a breach you report immediately, even if you recall the message. Photos of participants are records. They are not sent to a group, posted, or kept on a personal phone.",
  ],
  "AS-02|Incidents": [
    "Report a suspicious email you clicked, a lost device, a wrong recipient, or a login that is not yours. Early reporting is how the breach is contained. You will not be in more trouble for speaking quickly.",
    "Do not delete the evidence to tidy up. Leave the message, note the time, and follow the data breach process. Someone has to judge whether people must be told. That someone needs the facts.",
  ],
  "AS-03|Definitions": [
    "Sexual harassment is an unwelcome sexual advance, request, or conduct that a reasonable person would expect to offend, humiliate, or intimidate. It includes comments, images, jokes, and touching. Sex-based harassment is demeaning conduct because of someone's sex, even when it is not 'sexual'.",
    "Discrimination is less favourable treatment because of a protected attribute. Victimisation is punishing someone for complaining. A hostile workplace environment is one where that conduct is allowed to be the culture. Any of these can happen in an office, a group chat, or a participant's home.",
  ],
  "AS-03|In support settings": [
    "Harassment can come from participants and families, not only from colleagues. You still do not have to accept sexual comments, exposure that is targeted at you, or being touched. The person's disability does not make the behaviour your job to absorb. It does change how the response is planned.",
    "Balance their rights with your safety. The plan should say how this is handled: what you say, when you leave, who you tell. Do not invent a restrictive practice to manage it, and do not ignore it. Online harassment and roster-based harassment — shifts used as punishment, explicit messages — are the same conduct. Screenshot and report.",
  ],
  "AS-03|Responding": [
    "If you witness it and it is safe, a bystander can name it, support the person, or report it. Laughing along teaches the harasser it is fine. You do not have to be a hero. You do have to not join in.",
    "Report through the path that does not force you back to the person who did it, if that person is your supervisor. You are entitled to support and to as much confidentiality as the process allows. Ask what will happen next.",
  ],
  "AS-03|The positive duty": [
    "Employers have a positive duty to take reasonable and proportionate steps to prevent sexual harassment and related conduct, not only to respond after a complaint. Prevention is the work.",
    "That looks like leaders who model it, a culture where jokes are interrupted, a risk assessment that includes houses and group chats, a reporting path people trust, and monitoring of whether reports lead to change. A policy in a drawer is not the duty.",
  ],
  "AS-04|Fraud and claiming": [
    "Claiming for a support that was not delivered is fraud. So is claiming a longer shift than happened, or a more expensive support than was provided. Over-servicing and up-coding take a participant's budget for something they did not receive.",
    "A participant's NDIS number is not a tool you share so someone else can claim, and it is not something you use out of curiosity. If you are asked to sign a timesheet that is not true, you refuse and you tell someone outside that request.",
  ],
  "AS-04|Conflicts of interest": [
    "A conflict is when your interest, or your organisation's, could sway a decision that should be the participant's. Referring them to a related provider, or a support coordinator steering them toward the in-house service, is the common case.",
    "The conflict is declared before they choose, other options are offered where they exist, and the choice is recorded. Plan managers and coordinators have the same duty. A register that is never updated is not a declaration.",
  ],
  "AS-04|Gifts and benefits": [
    "Know the threshold your organisation sets. Below it, a small gesture may be acceptable if it is declared where required. Above it, or when it is cash, you decline. A gift from a provider who wants your referrals is not a gift. It is an influence.",
    "Decline kindly and record it. 'They would have been offended' is not a reason to keep something that ties your advice to their money.",
  ],
  "AS-04|Reporting": [
    "Report internally through the path that is not the person who benefits. Say what happened, who gained, and what records exist. Do it when you suspect, not when you can prove the whole scheme.",
    "Fraud and some conflicts also go to the NDIA or the Commission. You are protected when you report in good faith. If you are told to leave it alone, that instruction is part of what you report.",
  ],
  "CC-01|Anatomy and conditions": [
    "The bowel stores and moves stool. Nerves, muscles, fluid, and routine all affect it. A neurogenic bowel, common after spinal injury and in some neurological conditions, does not get the usual signals. Constipation is hard, infrequent stool. Impaction is a blockage of hard stool. Both can make someone very unwell, and in a person with a spinal injury they can trigger autonomic dysreflexia.",
    "You are learning the theory so you understand the plan. You do not diagnose, and you do not change the routine because yesterday was different. The plan is the instruction.",
  ],
  "CC-01|The bowel care plan": [
    "A qualified practitioner writes the bowel care plan: how often, which method, which products, and what to do if it does not work. Your name is not on that plan as the author.",
    "Follow the frequency and the method. Do not add an extra suppository, skip a day because the shift is busy, or swap products. If the plan is unclear or the result is not what it describes, stop and contact the person it names.",
  ],
  "CC-01|Procedures": [
    "Stoma care, suppositories, enemas, and digital stimulation or evacuation are done only if the plan includes them and you have been signed off to do them. Theory in this course is not that sign-off.",
    "Each procedure has a technique, a position, and a stop point. You use the ones you were shown for this person. Digital stimulation and evacuation can cause harm, including autonomic dysreflexia, if they are done outside the plan. If you have not been signed off, you do not 'have a go' because no one else is rostered.",
  ],
  "CC-01|Emergencies": [
    "Autonomic dysreflexia is a dangerous rise in blood pressure in some people with spinal cord injury. Signs include a pounding headache, sweating, flushing, a blocked nose, and goosebumps above the injury. A bowel problem can cause it. Sit them up if that is the emergency plan, find and relieve the cause only as you are trained, and call for emergency help. Do not lie them flat and wait.",
    "Bleeding, severe pain, or no result when the plan expected one are reasons to stop and contact the person named in the plan. You do not repeat the procedure to 'make it work'.",
  ],
  "CC-01|Dignity and records": [
    "Bowel care is intimate. Explain, get consent for this occasion, close the door, and cover what you can. Talk to them, not over them. Do not discuss their bowel in the kitchen.",
    "Record on the chart the plan uses, including the Bristol type if that is what the team tracks: what you did, the result, and how they tolerated it. Report pain, blood, a change, or a failed routine the same day. A blank day reads as 'nothing happened', which may be the problem.",
  ],
  "CC-02|Types and plans": [
    "A PEG goes into the stomach through the abdominal wall. A PEJ goes further, into the jejunum. A nasogastric tube goes through the nose into the stomach and is usually shorter term. They are not interchangeable. The feeding plan says which this person has, what formula, what rate or volume, and what to do if it will not flow.",
    "You follow that plan. You do not give a different feed, a different rate, or food by mouth because they seem hungry, unless the plan says oral intake is allowed. This course is theory. Practical sign-off is separate.",
  ],
  "CC-02|Feeding": [
    "A bolus is a volume given over a short time. A pump gives the feed at a set rate. Use the method, the volume, and the rate on the plan. Do not speed a pump up to finish before the end of your shift.",
    "Position them upright as the plan says, and keep them upright for the time it specifies, so the feed does not come back and enter the airway. Flush the tube with the amount and the type of water the plan names, before and after, so it does not block.",
  ],
  "CC-02|Medication via tube": [
    "Only medicines the plan or the pharmacist says can go down the tube are given that way. Some must not be crushed. Crushing a slow-release tablet can deliver the whole dose at once. If it is not on the list, you do not crush it.",
    "Give them one at a time and flush between them, with the volume the plan states. Mixing everything into the feed is how tubes block and how doses are lost. If you have not been signed off for this, you do not do it.",
  ],
  "CC-02|Site care": [
    "Clean the stoma site as the plan describes, and look at it while you do. Redness, swelling, ooze, smell, pain, or the person pulling at it can be infection. Granulation tissue is the extra moist tissue that can grow around the site. The plan says how it is managed. You do not cut it or treat it with something from the bathroom cupboard.",
    "Report changes the same day. A site that looks worse than yesterday is not something you 'keep an eye on' until the end of the week.",
  ],
  "CC-02|Problems": [
    "A blocked tube is managed with the flush and the steps in the plan. Do not force a syringe, and do not poke the tube with an object. A dislodged tube is an emergency of a different kind. Do not try to push it back in. Cover the site as taught and get help immediately. A nasogastric tube that may have moved is not used until placement is confirmed the way the plan requires.",
    "Aspiration signs include coughing, distress, a wet voice, or a change in breathing during or after a feed. Stop the feed and follow the emergency steps. Call 000 when they are in distress, when the tube is dislodged and you cannot reach the clinical contact, or whenever the plan says to.",
  ],
  "CC-03|Severe dysphagia": [
    "Severe dysphagia means the swallow is unsafe for ordinary food and fluid. The mechanism is poor coordination or sensation, so food or drink enters the airway. Silent aspiration is when that happens without a cough. You will not always see it.",
    "Repeated chest infections can be aspiration pneumonia. That is why the texture, the position, and the pace are not preferences. They are how this person breathes safely. This course builds on safe mealtimes. It does not replace the speech pathologist's plan or your practical sign-off.",
  ],
  "CC-03|Plans": [
    "The speech pathologist's plan names the IDDSI level, the utensils, the position, the cues, and what is never offered. Follow it at every intake, including medication with water and 'just a taste' of someone else's meal.",
    "Ask for a review when they cough more, get a chest infection, lose weight, sound wet after meals, or the plan no longer matches what they can do. You do not quietly thicken 'a bit more' as your own amendment.",
  ],
  "CC-03|IDDSI in detail": [
    "The flow test checks drink thickness. The fork and spoon tests check food. Use the test for the level on the plan, every time the texture could have changed: a new batch, a drink that sat, a meal that was reheated.",
    "Thickener is added as the plan and the product say. Stirring by eye is how drinks end up too thin. Too thin aspirates. The wrong texture of food can choke. If you cannot get the test to pass, do not serve it.",
  ],
  "CC-03|At the table": [
    "Position them upright, stable, and supported, as the plan shows, before any food or fluid. Keep them upright afterwards for the time specified. A bolus that is too big, or a pace that is too fast, overwhelms the swallow. Use the spoon and the cues in the plan.",
    "Fatigue makes the swallow worse. A long meal, a recline, or a conversation that distracts them can be the risk. Stop when the plan says to stop, even if there is food left. Offer the rest the way the plan allows, later.",
  ],
  "CC-03|Emergencies": [
    "Know the choking response you were trained in, and call 000 when they cannot cough, speak, or breathe. Do not give water to 'wash it down' if they are choking.",
    "After an event, watch them as the plan says. A cleared airway can still be followed by distress. Report every choking episode and every suspected aspiration so the plan is reviewed. Do not record it as 'coughed a bit, all fine' and move on.",
  ],
  "CC-04|Ventilation": [
    "Invasive ventilation uses a tube into the airway, such as a tracheostomy. Non-invasive ventilation uses a mask or similar interface. Both support breathing. The settings — pressures, rates, oxygen — are prescribed by a clinician. You do not turn a dial because the alarm is annoying or because they 'seem to want more air'.",
    "This course is theory. You do not support ventilation unless you have been signed off for this person and this equipment.",
  ],
  "CC-04|Equipment": [
    "Circuits, filters, and humidifiers do specific jobs. A dry airway and a blocked filter are both dangerous. Change and check them as the plan says, and know what a normal setup looks like so you can see when it is wrong.",
    "Batteries and backup power are part of the support, not a spare you discover during an outage. Know how long the battery lasts, how it is charged, and what you do when the power fails. Test that knowledge on a calm day.",
  ],
  "CC-04|Monitoring": [
    "Signs of distress include a change in breathing, colour, sweating, restlessness, drowsiness, and the person saying they cannot breathe. Trust those signs over a machine that looks normal, and trust a machine alarm over a wish that it is a false one.",
    "Oxygen saturation is monitored only as prescribed, with the device and the target in the plan. You do not add oxygen, and you do not chase a number by changing ventilator settings. You follow the response the plan gives for that reading, and you call when it says to call.",
  ],
  "CC-04|Alarms": [
    "A high-pressure alarm can mean a blockage, a cough, a kink, or water in the circuit. A low-pressure or disconnect alarm can mean the circuit has come apart or the mask has leaked. Know which sound is which for this machine.",
    "Troubleshoot in the order you were taught: look at the person first, then the connections, then the plan's steps. Do not silence an alarm and walk away. If you cannot fix it quickly, use the emergency plan.",
  ],
  "CC-04|Emergencies": [
    "Know where the manual resuscitation bag is and how you were taught to use it if the ventilator fails. Practise the location, not the panic. Call 000 early. Say that the person is ventilator dependent.",
    "The emergency plan names who to call, what equipment leaves with them, and what you do in a power failure. If you have not read it this shift, read it before you need it. Stay with the person. Do not leave them on a failing machine while you look for a supervisor.",
  ],
  "CC-05|Tracheostomy": [
    "A tracheostomy is an opening into the windpipe so a person can breathe, or so secretions can be cleared. The tube type — cuffed or uncuffed — changes how they breathe and whether they can speak. A speaking valve is used only as the plan allows. You do not put a valve on a cuff that is inflated unless the plan says that combination is safe.",
    "Why this person has the tube, and which type, is in the plan. You do not change tubes or sizes. Theory here is not the practical sign-off.",
  ],
  "CC-05|Suctioning": [
    "Suctioning clears secretions the person cannot clear. Indications are in the plan: the sound, the distress, the visible secretions. Suctioning because it is 'due' when they are clear can harm. Not suctioning when they are obstructed also harms.",
    "Technique, depth, and how long you suction are set by the plan and by the competency you were signed off for. Deeper and longer is not better. It can injure and it can drop their oxygen. If you are not signed off, you do not suction. You call someone who is.",
  ],
  "CC-05|Care": [
    "Inner cannula care, stoma cleaning, and ties are done as the plan describes, with the technique you were shown. Ties hold the tube in. They are not loosened for comfort in a way that lets the tube come out, and they are not left wet and dirty against the skin.",
    "Look at the skin every time. Redness, breakdown, bleeding, or a tube that sits differently from yesterday is reported. You do not pad a problem and hope the next shift notices.",
  ],
  "CC-05|Emergencies": [
    "A blocked tube is an emergency. Follow the plan: often that means removing the inner cannula if there is one, and the emergency steps you were signed off to do. A dislodged tube is also an emergency. Do not force it back in. Use the emergency kit at the bedside and call 000.",
    "Bleeding from the stoma or the tube is reported immediately and treated as the plan says. The emergency kit — spare tube, smaller tube, ties, suction, bag — stays at the bedside, complete, every shift. Check it when you arrive. A kit in a cupboard down the hall is not at the bedside.",
  ],
  "CC-05|Infection control": [
    "Some parts of tracheostomy care are clean technique and some are sterile, as you were taught for that step. The difference matters. A suction catheter that touches the sheet does not go into the airway.",
    "Equipment is single use or cleaned the way the plan says. Suction tubing, catheters, and the site are not 'close enough' if they are contaminated. Clean your hands at the moments this task requires, including after you finish.",
  ],
  "CC-06|Types": [
    "An indwelling urethral catheter stays in the bladder through the urethra. A suprapubic catheter enters through the abdomen. Intermittent catheterisation drains and is removed. They have different care and different risks. The plan says which this person has and who is signed off to change or insert it.",
    "You do not insert or change a catheter because this course explained the types. Insertion is a signed-off skill. Daily care is what the plan authorises you to do.",
  ],
  "CC-06|Daily care": [
    "Hygiene around the entry site follows the plan: what you clean with, and how. You do not disconnect the system for convenience. Securing the catheter stops it pulling. A tube under tension causes injury and can be pulled out.",
    "Fluid intake, as the plan encourages, keeps urine flowing. Notice if they are suddenly drinking much less, or if the output drops. That is a clinical change, not a quiet day.",
  ],
  "CC-06|Bags": [
    "Empty the bag before it is overfull, without letting the outlet touch the floor or the toilet, and close it after. The bag stays below the bladder so urine does not flow back.",
    "Change the bag on the schedule and with the clean technique you were shown. Positioning matters in bed and in a wheelchair: no kinks, no loops that pull, no bag in their lap above the bladder. A kinked tube is why bladders overfill.",
  ],
  "CC-06|Problems": [
    "No drainage can mean a kink, a full bag left closed, a blockage, or a dislodged catheter. Check the simple causes the plan lists, then get help. Do not flush a catheter unless you are authorised and the plan says to.",
    "Bypassing (urine leaking around the tube), blood, or signs of a urinary infection — fever, confusion, pain, foul urine, feeling unwell — are reported the same day. In a person at risk of autonomic dysreflexia, a blocked catheter is an emergency. Treat it with the urgency the plan gives it.",
  ],
  "CC-06|Records": [
    "Record output when the plan asks: amount, colour, and anything unusual. A sudden drop or a sudden blood stain is part of the note, not a detail you mention verbally and forget.",
    "Report pain, no drainage you cannot fix, blood, fever, and a catheter that has come out. Say when it started and what you have already checked. The next decision depends on that.",
  ],
  "CC-07|Authority": [
    "A subcutaneous injection is given only when it is prescribed and written on the medication authority or plan for this person. The plan names the medicine, the dose, the time, and the site guidance. Your confidence is not an authority.",
    "Scope is the injections you have been signed off to give. Insulin, an anticoagulant, and a hormone treatment are different skills in practice. This course is theory. Without the practical sign-off, you do not inject.",
  ],
  "CC-07|Preparation": [
    "Pens and syringes are used as the product and the plan describe. You check the name, the dose, the time, and the expiry against the chart, and you check you have the right person. Interruptions are when doses are doubled. Start again if you lose your place.",
    "Storage matters. Some of these medicines are refrigerated, and some pens in use are not. Follow the instruction for that medicine. A pen left in a hot car is not a dose you give.",
  ],
  "CC-07|Technique": [
    "Sites and rotation are in the plan. Using the same spot repeatedly damages the skin and changes how the medicine is absorbed. Angle and depth are what you were shown for that device. A subcutaneous injection is into the fatty layer, not a guess toward muscle.",
    "If you have not been shown this device, you do not learn it from the diagram alone. Ask for the sign-off. The person should know what you are doing, and they can refuse.",
  ],
  "CC-07|Sharps": [
    "The needle goes straight into a sharps container. You do not recap it, and you do not carry it across the room in your hand. A container that is full is replaced, not packed down.",
    "If you are stuck, wash, report, and get medical advice the same shift. Say what the needle had been used for. Do not decide it was clean and skip the report.",
  ],
  "CC-07|Adverse events": [
    "Hypoglycaemia can follow insulin. Know the signs — sweating, shaking, confusion, drowsiness — and the person's plan for treating it. Anaphylaxis is a severe allergic reaction: breathing difficulty, swelling, collapse. Follow the action plan and call 000.",
    "Site reactions — redness, swelling, rash — are recorded and reported, especially if they are new or spreading. Do not keep injecting into a site that looks infected. Stop and ask.",
  ],
  "CC-08|Wounds": [
    "Wounds differ. A pressure injury, a diabetic ulcer, a venous ulcer, and a surgical wound are not dressed the same way. Healing moves through stages, and a wound can stall or go backwards. You are not diagnosing the type. You are following the plan for the wound this person has.",
    "If the wound does not match the description in the plan — deeper, wetter, a new smell — stop and get the review. Do not keep applying yesterday's dressing to a wound that has changed.",
  ],
  "CC-08|Plans": [
    "The practitioner's plan names the dressing, how often, how it is cleaned, and what the wound should look like. Frequency is clinical. Leaving a dressing for an extra day because the shift is busy is a change to the plan you are not allowed to make.",
    "Photograph and measure only as the plan and the consent allow, the same way each time, so a change is visible. Photos are health information. They go in the record, not on a phone.",
  ],
  "CC-08|Technique": [
    "Aseptic non-touch technique means the key parts — the dressing surface that touches the wound, the sterile equipment — are not touched by your hands, your clothes, or the bed. If they are, you start with new equipment.",
    "Remove and apply dressings the way you were shown. This course does not sign you off to do it. If you are not signed off, you do not 'have a look and redress' because the dressing has come loose. You make it safe and get someone who is authorised.",
  ],
  "CC-08|Pressure injuries": [
    "Pressure injuries are staged by how deep they are. You use the stage in the plan. You do not re-stage a wound from memory of a poster. Prevention is repositioning on the schedule, the right mattress and cushion, clean dry skin, and nutrition as the plan describes.",
    "Equipment only works if it is the equipment assessed for them and it is switched on and checked. A pressure mattress that is flat is not working. Report it the same hour.",
  ],
  "CC-08|Escalation": [
    "Infection signs include increasing pain, redness, warmth, swelling, smell, ooze, fever, or the person becoming unwell. Deterioration is a wound that is larger, deeper, or newly black. Both are reported the same day, and sooner if they are acutely unwell.",
    "Say what you saw, when it changed, and what dressing is in place. Call 000 if they are severely unwell. A wound review can wait until morning only when the plan's escalation path says it can.",
  ],
  "ST-01|Epilepsy": [
    "Seizures are not all the same. Some are convulsive. Some are a blank stare, a strange movement, or a change in awareness. The person's plan names their seizure types. Learn those, not a generic picture.",
    "Triggers may include missed medication, illness, missed sleep, stress, flashing light, or missed meals. They are individual. SUDEP is sudden unexpected death in epilepsy. It is why seizures are taken seriously, why plans exist, and why you do not leave someone alone in the recovery position and walk off.",
  ],
  "ST-01|Seizure first aid": [
    "Time the seizure from the start. Protect them from injury: something soft under the head, hazards moved away. Do not hold them down and do not put anything in the mouth. When the convulsion stops, the recovery position helps breathing, if that is what you were taught and they are breathing.",
    "Call 000 when the plan says to, when you do not know them, when the seizure is longer than the plan's limit, when a second seizure starts, when they are injured, pregnant, or not breathing normally, or when you are unsure. Stay until they are fully recovered or help has taken over.",
  ],
  "ST-01|Plans": [
    "The epilepsy management plan describes their seizures, triggers, first aid, and when to call. The emergency medication management plan describes if and when a medicine such as midazolam is given. Read both before the shift. They are not the same document.",
    "If there is no plan and the person is known to have epilepsy, ask for it before you are the only one there. Do not build an emergency response from memory of a different participant.",
  ],
  "ST-01|Midazolam": [
    "Emergency midazolam is given only when the emergency medication plan's indications are met: usually a seizure that has gone on for the time the plan states. Early, late, or 'to be safe' are not the indication. The route and the dose are the ones written for this person. You do not use a dose you remember from someone else.",
    "You only give it if you are authorised and trained for this plan. After it is given, watch breathing and responsiveness as the plan says, because the medicine can sedate. Call an ambulance when the plan says to. Do not leave them alone afterwards.",
  ],
  "ST-01|Records": [
    "A seizure chart records the time, how long, what the seizure looked like, what you did, and how they were afterwards. That pattern is how the treating team adjusts care. 'Had a seizure' is not a chart.",
    "Report as the plan requires, including clusters, injuries, and any dose of emergency medication. If you gave midazolam, the record includes the time and the dose from the plan, and who was told.",
  ],
  "ST-02|Asthma": [
    "Asthma narrows the airways and fills them with mucus, so breathing out becomes hard. A trigger — a virus, smoke, exercise, pollen, a cold house — can start it. This person's triggers are in their action plan.",
    "You cannot see how tight an airway is from across the room at the start. Learn their early signs: cough, wheeze, saying they are tight, waking at night. The plan is more useful than a general impression.",
  ],
  "ST-02|Action plans": [
    "Asthma action plans use zones. The well zone is everyday treatment. The worsening zone is what you add when symptoms start. The severe zone is an emergency. The colours and the words differ by plan. Read this person's.",
    "Medications are the preventer they take every day and the reliever for symptoms. You do not swap them, skip the preventer because they seem well, or borrow another person's puffer. The plan names which inhaler and how many puffs.",
  ],
  "ST-02|First aid": [
    "Sit them upright. Do not lie them down. Follow the national first aid steps you were taught: four puffs of reliever, one puff at a time, with four breaths after each puff, wait four minutes, and repeat if they are not better. Use a spacer if that is what the plan and the training say.",
    "If they are not improving, or they are severe from the start, call 000 and keep giving the reliever as the first aid steps say. Stay with them. An asthma attack that is 'a bit better' can still worsen in the car on the way to somewhere other than hospital.",
  ],
  "ST-02|Severe attacks": [
    "Severe signs include struggling to breathe, unable to speak a sentence, blue lips, collapsing, or a reliever that is not working. That is 000, not a wait to see if the next four minutes help. Say it is asthma.",
    "Anaphylaxis can look similar and can trigger asthma. If they have an adrenaline plan and the signs fit allergy — swelling, known allergen, collapse — follow that plan as you were trained. Do not choose between the plans from guesswork. If you are unsure, call 000 and follow the emergency instructions.",
  ],
  "ST-03|ABI": [
    "Acquired brain injury happens after birth, from trauma, stroke, hypoxia, infection, or other causes. Which part of the brain was injured changes the effect: movement, speech, memory, impulse, vision, or personality. Two people with an ABI are not the same support.",
    "It is often invisible. Someone may walk and talk and still be unable to plan, remember, or control an impulse. Do not treat a good conversation as proof they can manage the whole task alone.",
  ],
  "ST-03|Cognitive effects": [
    "Memory problems mean you do not rely on 'I told them this morning'. Write it down, show it, and repeat without irritation. Attention problems mean one instruction at a time, in a quieter space.",
    "Executive function is starting, sequencing, and stopping. They may know the steps and still not begin. Fatigue is neurological, not laziness. A person can be capable at 9 and unable at 2. Build the day around that, rather than pushing through and calling the result behaviour.",
  ],
  "ST-03|Behaviour and emotion": [
    "Disinhibition can look like swearing, sexual comments, or saying the quiet part out loud. It is an injury effect, not a personality you punish. You respond calmly, redirect, and do not shame them in front of others.",
    "Irritability may come from fatigue, noise, or too many demands. Reduced insight means they may not see the risk you see. You still involve them in decisions. You do not take over their life because insight is patchy. You adjust the support and explain the risk in the moment.",
  ],
  "ST-03|Strategies": [
    "Routine reduces the load on memory and planning. Keep the order of the day predictable, and warn them before a change. External memory aids — a phone alarm, a whiteboard, a list they helped write — are the support. Your memory is not the system.",
    "Pace activities. Rest is part of the plan, not a reward. Family and identity matter. They had a life before the injury. Ask what still matters, and do not speak about them as if the injury is the whole person.",
  ],
  "ST-04|Autism": [
    "Autism is a difference in how a person senses, communicates, and thinks. It is a spectrum of traits, not a line from mild to severe, and it is an identity many people claim with pride. Language follows the person: many prefer 'autistic person'. Ask.",
    "Co-occurring conditions are common, including anxiety, epilepsy, and ADHD. Do not explain every need as 'the autism'. Read the plan for this person. Another autistic person you supported is not the template.",
  ],
  "ST-04|Sensory": [
    "Hypersensitivity means sound, light, touch, smell, or taste is intense or painful. Hyposensitivity means they may not register pain, temperature, or body position the way you expect. Both can be true in the same person, for different senses.",
    "Change the environment: lower the noise, dim the light, warn before touch, offer the clothing they can bear. Stimming — rocking, flapping, humming — is often regulation. You do not stop it because it looks odd. You only intervene if they are about to be hurt.",
  ],
  "ST-04|Communication": [
    "Some people use speech, some use a device, pictures, or behaviour. Literal language means sarcasm and hints fail. Say what you mean. Processing time means you ask, then wait. Repeating louder is not waiting.",
    "Masking is when they hide autistic traits to get through a social situation. It is exhausting and it can look like they are 'fine' until they are not. Do not praise them for masking, and do not require eye contact. Communication is successful when they understood you and you understood them, not when they looked at your eyes.",
  ],
  "ST-04|Distress": [
    "A meltdown is an overload response. It can look like shouting, crying, or hitting. A shutdown is overload that goes quiet: still, unable to speak, withdrawn. Neither is a tantrum you discipline, and neither is solved by more demands.",
    "Prevention is the sensory and communication work earlier in the day. In the moment, reduce input, reduce language, keep them safe, and follow the plan. Do not restrain them to stop the meltdown. Restraint makes overload worse and it may be a restrictive practice.",
  ],
  "ST-04|Harmful practices": [
    "Compliance-based programs that train a person to obey, to sit still, or to perform 'quiet hands' teach them to ignore their own distress. That is harmful. Forced eye contact is harmful. Stopping a stim that is not hurting anyone is harmful.",
    "Restrictive practices are not autism strategies. They are regulated, last resort, and authorised, or they do not happen. If a program, a school, or a colleague asks you to hold someone down or lock a door to make them comply, you refuse and you report it.",
  ],
  "ST-05|Bipolar disorder": [
    "Bipolar disorder involves episodes of depression and episodes of elevated mood. Types differ in how high the mood goes and how the episodes are patterned. Mania is a marked high: little sleep, racing speech, grand plans, risk-taking. Hypomania is a milder high that can still wreck money, relationships, and judgement. Depression is the low: slowed, hopeless, sometimes suicidal.",
    "The recovery model means the person leads the life they want, with support, including between episodes. You are not treating the illness. You are supporting the person through the day, using their plan.",
  ],
  "ST-05|Early signs": [
    "Early signs are personal. Less sleep, more spending, faster speech, irritability, or withdrawal from people they usually see. Their wellness plan or relapse plan lists their signs. Read it. 'They seem happy' can be the start of a high.",
    "When you see the signs, follow the plan: who to tell, what to reduce, what not to argue about. Early action is kinder than a crisis response three days later.",
  ],
  "ST-05|Everyday support": [
    "Routine protects sleep and meals, which protect mood. Help the routine happen without turning it into surveillance. Medication support follows the medication plan. You do not suggest they stop because they feel well. Feeling well can be the medicine working, or the start of a high.",
    "Choice and control stay. An episode does not cancel their rights. You may need to slow a risky decision down and involve the person named in the plan. You do not take their card, their phone, or their weekend away unless the plan and a proper authority say so.",
  ],
  "ST-05|Risk": [
    "Suicide risk rises in depression and can also be present in mixed states. Take talk of death, giving things away, or sudden calm after deep despair seriously. Ask, and follow the crisis plan. Do not leave them alone with the means if the plan says to stay.",
    "Mania can bring impulsive spending, sexual risk, and driving. Say what you are worried about, plainly, and contact the person the plan names. Escalate the same day when the risk is current. A note for the next review is not an escalation.",
  ],
  "ST-06|PTSD": [
    "PTSD can follow trauma. Symptom clusters include reliving the event, avoiding reminders, negative changes in thoughts and mood, and being constantly on guard. Complex PTSD can also include difficulties with emotion, self-worth, and relationships, often after prolonged trauma.",
    "It is common among people with disability, including people who lived in institutions or were harmed by people who were meant to care for them. You may be supporting someone for whom a uniform, a bathroom, or a raised voice is a reminder. This course sits alongside trauma-informed support. It does not make you their therapist.",
  ],
  "ST-06|Recognising episodes": [
    "A flashback is the person re-experiencing the trauma now. They may not be fully in the room with you. Dissociation is a disconnect: blank, unreachable, or 'not here'. Hyperarousal is jumpiness, anger, scanning for danger. Avoidance is refusing places, topics, or care that remind them.",
    "Do not grab them, shout, or demand they 'come back'. Use their name, orient them gently to where they are, and follow the plan. What looks like refusal of personal care may be avoidance of a trigger. Force is the worst response.",
  ],
  "ST-06|Support": [
    "Grounding brings attention to the present: feet on the floor, a cold drink, naming five things they can see, if those are strategies they have agreed. Do not invent a graphic exercise. Use what their plan or they have said helps.",
    "Safety and choice first. Ask before touch. Offer a way out of the room. Do not ask them to tell you the story of what happened. You are not collecting it, and telling it can re-traumatise. If they volunteer it, listen, and do not press for details.",
  ],
  "ST-06|Environment": [
    "Learn their triggers from the plan, not by testing them. Change what you can: who is in the room, the noise, the surprise, the way personal care is done. Predictability is treatment-adjacent support. Say what will happen next.",
    "Work with the treating team through the proper channel. Do not ring a psychologist for a chat, and do not change a therapy strategy because it is inconvenient on shift. If a strategy in the house is making things worse, feed that back through the plan's review path.",
  ],
  "ST-07|Schizophrenia": [
    "Schizophrenia affects thinking, perception, emotion, and motivation. Symptoms can include delusions, hallucinations, disorganised thinking, and what are called negative symptoms: flat expression, little speech, withdrawal, difficulty starting. The course varies. Many people live well with treatment and support between episodes.",
    "Stigma is part of what you are there to not add. Do not joke about 'being psycho', do not assume they are dangerous, and do not talk about them as their diagnosis. Read the plan for this person.",
  ],
  "ST-07|Responding to psychosis": [
    "If they are describing a delusion or a hallucination, you do not argue the content and you do not pretend you can see or hear it. You validate the feeling. 'That sounds frightening' is useful. 'That is not real' is a fight you will not win, and it can wreck trust.",
    "Keep the scene safe. Reduce stimulation. Give space. Use the de-escalation in the plan. If they are at risk, or someone else is, follow the crisis steps and call for help. Stay calm. A room full of workers closing in is not calming.",
  ],
  "ST-07|Recovery support": [
    "Routine, sleep, meals, and something meaningful in the day are the support. Negative symptoms can look like laziness. They are not. Offer, wait, and do the step with them rather than taking the day over or abandoning them to a dark room.",
    "Physical health matters. Some medicines affect weight, movement, and heart risk. Notice the side effects the plan names — stiffness, restlessness, thirst — and report them. You do not suggest they stop the medicine because of a side effect. You get that information to the prescriber through the proper path.",
  ],
  "ST-07|Relapse and risk": [
    "Early warning signs are individual: poorer sleep, withdrawing, more suspicion, stopping medication, talking more to voices. The relapse plan lists theirs. Act on the list, not on a feeling that they seem 'a bit odd'.",
    "A crisis plan says who to call, when to call 000, and what helps. Call when they are at risk of harm, when you are, or when the plan's line is crossed. Do not wait overnight on a serious risk because you did not want to overreact.",
  ],
  "ST-08|Coercive control": [
    "Coercive control is a pattern of domination: isolation, monitoring, humiliation, control of money, threats, and rules, not only physical hits. One incident may look small. The pattern is the harm. The person may defend the controller and still be unsafe.",
    "Laws differ by state. Some recognise coercive control as an offence. You do not need to know the charge to take the pattern seriously. Your organisation's procedure says how you record and refer. Follow it.",
  ],
  "ST-08|Disability and risk": [
    "The perpetrator may be the carer, the partner, a parent, or a worker. Dependence makes leaving harder. The person may rely on the controller for personal care, communication, or a place to live. They may not be believed because of their disability.",
    "Control can run through the support itself: speaking for them, withholding the wheelchair or the communication device, cancelling workers they trust, managing all the money, refusing to leave the room during personal care. Watch who is afraid of whom.",
  ],
  "ST-08|Responding": [
    "Safety first. Do not confront the perpetrator. Confrontation can escalate to serious harm after you leave. Talk with the person alone, if you can do that safely, and believe the pattern.",
    "Use the referral pathway: domestic violence services that can work with people with disability, police when there is immediate danger, and your provider's safeguarding lead. Offer the referral. Do not force them to leave on your timetable. Do not promise you can keep them safe by yourself.",
  ],
  "ST-08|Records": [
    "Write facts. What you saw and the words used. 'He checks her phone and told her she cannot see her sister. She said she is not allowed.' Not 'the boyfriend is toxic'. Facts are what a later protection order or investigation can use.",
    "Report through the safeguarding path the same day you form the concern. Do not store it in a personal note. If the controller is a worker, it is also an incident about that worker. Do not tip them off that a report is coming.",
  ],
}

